WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.169 --> 00:00:03.480
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:10.790
This paper describes a new technique to visualize
the inner workings of a generator neural network.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:14.540
This is a neural network that is is able to
create images for us.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:19.740
The key idea here is dissecting this neural
network, and looking for agreements between

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:27.730
a set of neurons and concepts in the output
image, such as trees, sky, clouds, and more.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:31.980
This means analyzing that these neurons are
responsible for buildings to appear in the

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:34.879
image, and those will generate clouds.

00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:40.530
Interestingly, such agreements can be found,
which means way more than just creating a

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:47.329
visualizations like this, because it enables
us to edit images without any artistic skills.

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:49.499
And now, hold on to your papers.

00:00:49.499 --> 00:00:55.729
The editing part works by forcefully activating
and deactivating these units and correspond

00:00:55.729 --> 00:00:59.620
to adding or removing these objects from an
image.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:00.620
And look!

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:05.390
This means that we can take an already existing
image, and ask this technique to remove trees

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:12.030
from it, or perhaps add more, the same with
domes, doors, and more.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.159
Wow!

00:01:13.159 --> 00:01:16.990
This is pretty cool, but you haven’t seen
the best part yet!

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:22.560
Note that so far, the amount of control we
have over the image is quite limited.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.329
And fortunately, we can take this further,
and select a region of the image where we

00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:29.350
wish to add something new.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:32.590
This is suddenly so much more granular and
useful!

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:38.530
The algorithm seems to understand that trees
need to be rooted somewhere and not just appear

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.229
from thin air.

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:41.530
Most of the time anyway.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:47.299
Interestingly, it also understands that bricks
don’t really belong here, but if I add it

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:53.390
to the side of the building, it continues
it in a way that is consistent with its appearance.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.130
Most of the time anyway.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:59.420
And of course, it is not perfect, here you
can see me struggling with this spaghetti

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:06.139
monster floating in the air that used to be
a tree, and it just refuses to be overwritten.

00:02:06.139 --> 00:02:11.730
And this is a very important lesson - most
research works are but a step in a thousand-mile

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:16.300
journey, and each of them tries to improve
upon the previous paper.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:21.380
This means that a few more papers down the
line, this will probably take place in HD,

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:25.599
perhaps in real-time, and with much higher
quality.

00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:31.110
This work also builds on previous knowledge
on generative adversarial networks, and whatever

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:36.489
the follow-up papers will contain, they will
build on knowledge that was found in this

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:37.489
work.

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:40.430
Welcome to the wonderful world of research!

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:45.300
And now, we can all rejoice because the authors
kindly made the source code available free

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:52.019
for everyone, and not only that, but there
is also a web app so you can also try it yourself!

00:02:52.019 --> 00:02:56.390
This is an excellent way of maximizing the
impact of your research work.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:03:01.170
Let the experts improve upon it by releasing
the source code, and let people play with

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.150
it, even laymen!

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:08.350
You will also find many failure cases, but
also cases where it works well, and I think

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:13.290
there is value in reporting both so we learn
a little more about this amazing algorithm.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.780
So, let’s do a little research together!

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:21.819
Make sure to post your results in the comments
section, I have a feeling that lots of high-quality

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:25.010
entertainment materials will surface very
soon.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:30.879
I bet the authors will also be grateful for
the feedback as well, so, let’s the experiments

00:03:30.879 --> 00:03:31.879
begin!

00:03:31.879 --> 00:03:34.469
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

